<title>BELGIUM: Capital gain helps Bekaert to 1996 profit.</title>
<headline>Capital gain helps Bekaert to 1996 profit.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year to December 31, 1996</p>
<p>(in billions of Belgian francs unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit		  2.28   vs   3.01</p>
<p>    Cons current profit *(1)	1.90   vs   3.18</p>
<p>    Cons operating profit	   0.77   vs   3.89</p>
<p>    Cons extraordinary items *(2) 1.38   vs   0.04</p>
<p>    Taxes				 0.12   vs   0.73</p>
<p>    Cons turnover		    61.82   vs  60.67</p>
<p>    Operating costs		  56.77   vs  52.93</p>
<p>    Cons cash flow *(3)	     5.97   vs   6.18</p>
<p>    Net dividend (francs)	    450   vs    450</p>
<p>NOTE - Belgian Bekaert NV is the world's largest independent maker of steel wire and cord products.</p>
<p>(1) Cons current profit excludes 2.31 billion franc capital gain on July sale of 10.1 million shares in Japanese BMA and provision of 1.93 billion francs for restructuring.</p>
<p>(2) Cons extraordinary items comprises extraordinary revenue of 2.64 billion francs (mainly from BMA sale) and extraordinary charges linked to restructuring of 1.26 billion francs.</p>
<p>(3) Cash flow was 8.23 billion francs including BMA sale. Excluding BMA sale, cash flow was helped by higher depreciation.</p>
<p>-- Nick Antonovics, Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6817, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
</text>
